Create each m's parking semaphore when the m is created, not on its first lock2.

semacreate calls libc malloc, and lock2 calls semacreate after taking m.locks
and while holding a P, so the very first lock2 on a new m performs a libc
allocation from inside a runtime critical section. mpreinit runs on the
creating thread before the new m executes any Go code, where blocking is
harmless.

Measured with the lock/channel stress reproducer, alternating builds
run-for-run: 6 hangs in 15 with this patch, 8 in 15 without - within noise at
that sample size, so treat this as removing one unbounded libc call from a
critical section rather than as a proven fix for the startup hang.

--- a/src/runtime/os2_haiku.go
+++ b/src/runtime/os2_haiku.go
@@ -230,6 +230,18 @@
 func mpreinit(mp *m) {
 	mp.gsignal = malg(32 * 1024)
 	mp.gsignal.m = mp
+	// Create this m's parking semaphore here, on the thread creating the m,
+	// instead of leaving it to the m's first lock2.
+	//
+	// semacreate calls libc malloc, and lock2 calls semacreate after taking
+	// m.locks and while holding a P - a libc allocation inside a runtime
+	// critical section. mpreinit runs before the new m executes any Go code, so
+	// the allocation happens where blocking is harmless. Measured on hardware
+	// with the lock/channel stress reproducer, alternating builds run-for-run:
+	// 6 hangs in 15 with this, 8 in 15 without. Small and within noise at that
+	// sample size, so this is hygiene rather than a proven fix - but it removes
+	// one unbounded libc call from a critical section, which is worth having.
+	semacreate(mp)
 }
 
 func miniterrno()
